    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>You have been seeing someone for a while. You message every day, you have been on a few dates, and it feels good. But you do not know what you are to each other, and the question keeps slipping into your head. Learning when and how to define the relationship can save you weeks of guessing and a lot of anxiety. It is also much less scary than it sounds.</p>
<p>This guide explains when the right moment is, what to think about before you ask, how to talk about exclusivity, and gives you scripts you can adjust and use. It also covers what to do when the answer is not the one you hoped for.</p>

<h2>Why the what are we talk is worth having</h2>
<p>Without a clear conversation, two people can be in very different places and never know it. One may think they are building something serious, while the other thinks it is a casual friendship. Neither is wrong to feel what they feel, but silence leaves room for hurt and misunderstanding.</p>
<p>Being clear is a kindness. It lets you both choose with full information, and it shows respect for the other person. A relationship that can survive an honest conversation is much stronger than one that depends on avoiding it.</p>

<h2>When to define the relationship</h2>
<p>There is no magic date, but some signs suggest it is a good time:</p>
<ul>
<li>You have spent real time together, on several dates and not only through messages.</li>
<li>You talk or text most days and look forward to it.</li>
<li>You find yourself wondering whether they are seeing other people.</li>
<li>You have started introducing each other to friends, or making plans a few weeks ahead.</li>
<li>You know what you want, even if only roughly, and the uncertainty is starting to bother you.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you have only chatted a few times or have not met in person, it may be too early. Spend more time getting to know each other first. Our guide to <a href="/blog/first-date-conversation-topics">first date conversation topics</a> can help you learn what matters to each other along the way.</p>

<h2>Get clear on what you want first</h2>
<p>Before you start the conversation, spend a few minutes with yourself. It is hard to ask for something you have not defined. Ask yourself:</p>
<ol>
<li>Do I want a serious, committed relationship with this person?</li>
<li>Do I want to be exclusive, or am I still exploring?</li>
<li>What would make me feel secure, and what would make me feel uneasy?</li>
<li>What will I do if they want something different?</li>
</ol>
<p>The last question matters most. Decide ahead of time what you are willing to accept. It is easier to stay calm in the conversation when you know where your own line is.</p>

<h2>Choosing the right moment and place</h2>
<p>Timing changes how the conversation feels. Pick a relaxed moment, not in the middle of an argument, after a long day or when one of you is rushing out. A quiet walk, a meal or a phone call at a calm hour all work well. Avoid raising it in front of friends or by dropping a heavy question into a casual text.</p>
<p>If you feel nervous, give a gentle heads up: "I would love to chat about where we are at, are you free tonight?" This stops it feeling like an ambush and gives the other person time to think. If you are not sure how to handle a first reaction, our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-handle-rejection-gracefully">how to handle rejection gracefully</a> will help whatever the answer is.</p>

<h2>Scripts you can copy for the what are we talk</h2>
<p>Words are easier when you have a starting point. Adjust these to your own voice:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The gentle opener.</strong> "I really enjoy spending time with you. I would like to know how you see us, and I will tell you how I feel too."</li>
<li><strong>The direct but kind version.</strong> "I am not seeing anyone else, and I would like us to be exclusive. How do you feel about that?"</li>
<li><strong>The checking-in version.</strong> "We have been seeing each other for a while now. What are you looking for at this point?"</li>
<li><strong>If you want to take it slow.</strong> "I like you a lot and I am not seeing anyone else. I am not ready to put a label on it yet, but I wanted to be honest."</li>
<li><strong>If you want something serious.</strong> "I am looking for a committed relationship. I would like to see whether that is where we are heading."</li>
</ul>
<p>Speak about your own feelings rather than accusing. "I feel unsure where we stand" lands much softer than "You never tell me what is going on." Then stop talking and listen.</p>

<h2>Talking about exclusivity clearly</h2>
<p>Exclusivity simply means you are both seeing only each other. It does not always come with a label, and different people understand it differently. That is why it is worth saying out loud, rather than assuming.</p>
<p>Be specific about what you mean. Does it include chatting on dating platforms? Should you delete your profile, pause it or leave it? Some couples agree to turn off their profiles together as a small, clear signal. Others discuss it more gradually. Whatever you decide, make sure you both mean the same thing. Mixed assumptions cause far more pain than an honest conversation.</p>

<h2>What to do with their answer</h2>
<p>A conversation like this can go three ways. They may feel the same, which is lovely, and you can celebrate. They may need time, in which case you can ask kindly what that looks like, and set a gentle limit for yourself. Or they may want something different, which hurts but is better to know now.</p>
<p>If they say they need time, listen for specifics. "I am just finishing a busy season at work and want to take it slowly" is different from "I do not do labels" said over and over for months. Trust patterns, not promises. If you find yourself waiting indefinitely and feeling worse, it is fair to step back. Check our guide to <a href="/blog/green-flags-and-red-flags-in-early-dating">green flags and red flags in early dating</a> to see how their behaviour compares with their words.</p>
<p>Whatever happens, stay kind. Do not argue, bargain or beg. You are allowed to want clarity, and you are allowed to leave if you cannot get it.</p>

<h2>Mistakes to avoid in this conversation</h2>
<p>A few common slips can turn a simple talk into a stressful one:</p>
<ul>
<li>Raising it in the middle of an argument or when one of you is upset.</li>
<li>Using ultimatums, such as "Commit now or we are done", before you have shared how you feel.</li>
<li>Reading too much into one answer instead of looking at how they behave over time.</li>
<li>Asking by text and then reading meaning into emojis or slow replies.</li>
<li>Telling them what they feel instead of asking.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep your tone curious and warm. You are not trying to win, only to understand where you both stand. If the first conversation does not settle everything, you can have a second one a few days later, once you have both had time to think.</p>

<h2>After the talk</h2>
<p>If you both decide to be together, small habits will keep the clarity going. Check in now and then, say thank you, and talk about little things before they become big ones. You might also begin to meet each other's friends, and eventually your families, which you can read about in <a href="/blog/introducing-your-partner-to-family-in-kenya">introducing your partner to family in Kenya</a>.</p>
<p>If you are still meeting people and not yet at this stage, that is fine too. You can <a href="/browse">browse people near you</a> and take things at your own pace, or <a href="/register">create your free profile</a> to start a new connection with clear intentions from the start.</p>
